Z||000025||Black88GT||06-12-2001||06:06 PM||||OK first of all, any type of torture is unconstitutional, and if you truly support torturing someone you are no better then they are.  "Eye for an Eye, Tooth for a Tooth" is Hammurabi's code, is this "advanced technological" society ready to adapt the FIRST written law in human history?  Why don't we legalize slavery while we are at it.<P>Secondly, if half the supporters of the death penalty were educated on the way the justice system works, they would be against it.  It's well known people who can't afford a "Dream Team" of lawyers have a much better chance of getting the death penalty then those who can.  This goes for famous people too.  Also, they way capitol punishment is used now, its neither a deterrent, a punishment, or a form of rehabilitation (the basis of the criminal justice system).  <P>I also laugh when I hear people talk about how they don't want to pay to keep him alive in prison.  It costed 100 million dollars to execute McVeigh (lawyers time, court fees, training for the execution etc.)  On average it costs around $20,000 per inmate for 1 year to feed/clothe them.  Do the math, it would be cheaper to just let him stay in prison the rest of his life.  It costs millions and millions for even low profile death penalty cases due to years of appeals.  Not to mention the Gov't wastes so much money as it is, there are more injustices in Gov't spending to get upset about (i.e. the Clintons trips to foreign countries with 300 guests coming along).<P>Lastly, I like how the liberal media now considers unborn babies human when they want to up the fatality number, but when they talk about abortion its always about how the baby isn't born yet so its not human.  Just a little observation. ||||reg||1
