Z||000034||Ken Wittlief||06-14-2001||10:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I have NO sympathy for criminals. I think they should die an excruciating slow death.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>  I know this thread is intended to be humorous (a good example of gallows humor BTW - coping with a grim situation]<P>  but if I can be serious for a second. Take the quote here, and replace 'criminals' with 'government agents' and you know what was in McVeighs head.<P>  replace it with 'students and teachers' and you know what was in the heads of students that went postal<P>   somewhere, sometime this cycle has to stop. And Im pretty sure it will have to be the sane people that break it, the people who have the upper hand, and the power, and the justification to kill, who stand up and say, "We choose to NOT kill because this HAS to stop!"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000035||Butter||06-14-2001||12:19 PM||||Went down threw this thread and I see a lot of needless torture.  What we need is productive torture.  My opinion on this would be to have some penal institution open up a couple new departments.  Have the research and development department put in charge of finding out why such a crime could happen so society could institute a way to avoid the crime.  After a given period of R & D on the condemned for the above purpose he then gets to go to the medical research department where the condemned could play guinea pig for the rest of his life in helping society cure some disease.  Now if the condemned proves to be more that the penal institution can control then we offer the condemned this alternative.  We put him in a locked room and provide him with only air.  We tell him to show us how to build a better society and see how he does.  Before he dies he will surely summarize that it would be better to try to improve on the society we have as aposed to trying to start all over.  After he dies we list his cause of death as natural causes due to his almost total independence from our society (we would be supplying his air and room).  This would make the statement: “Put to the Test” more of a deterrent than “Put to Death”.  I believe in an eye for an eye but T.M. didn’t have but two eyes to offer.  Util we can figure out how to institute a productive way to condemn a hard criminal I guess we must eliminate them.  <P>Just a different thought on the subject…..<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000036||LZeitgeist||06-14-2001||01:10 PM||||O.K., I just had to put a couple extra cents in here...<P>I'm becoming pretty appalled at some of these posts advocating torture... I'm definitely not defending TM in any way, shape or form - I'm glad they put him to death - but the capacity for a human mind to come up with ways to cause excruciating pain to another living being just disgusts me, and the fact that there are actually people in this world that *do* some of these things (nobody on this list, I'm sure) makes me want to throw up if I dwell on it for too long. (Read up on some medieval torture methods that were ACTUALLY USED if you'd like an education into the depravity of some human minds.)<P>I'm all for 'an eye for an eye', but the fact that these torture/revenge posts are coming from people that I like to think of as friends is pretty disturbing... I know, I don't have to read it, and nobody made me... but I just wanted to insert a little bit of a 'reality check' here... that's all. Nothing more.<P>Just my two cents... ||||reg||1
