Z||000038||Ken Wittlief||06-14-2001||02:12 PM||||if you havent figured it out yet, fairport is a suburb of rochester, home of Kodak.<P>  A former Kodak employee showed me a copy of a letter that was sent to the CEO. The administrator who opens all his mail copied it before she gave it to him, and the copy has since been copied many times.<P>   I dont have it, but in essence, some guy was convinced he use to know someone that is a real vampire. He was helping the guy move, and the guy was standing behind a truck, and when he looked in the mirror he could not see him, but when he turned around and looked the guy was there. He was convinced he could not see this guys reflection in a mirror - so the guy must be a vampire - so vampires must exist.<P>   after he had some time to stew over this he came to realize there must be some sort of light energy that we can see with our eyes, but that wont reflect off glass - and for some reasons vampires convert normal light into this new energy form, or they produce it some how, or they exist in a different dimension.<BR> <BR>  if true, then this is a major scientific discovery. There could be all kinds of energy out in space that we have never seen, cause all our telescopes and cameras use glass and mirrors - somehow only human eyeballs, and maybe animal eyeballs, can see this energy.<P>  so the purpose of his letter to Kodak was, he wanted them to fund his efforts to track this guy down, the vampire, and capture him, or another one, so the scientists at Kodak can study the light energy he emits, and figure out what it is.<P>   He also suggested the possibility that NASA would be interested in this, and maybe a new form of telescope could be developed, one that uses hundreds of human eyes as the collectors. He suggested prisoners with life sentances would be a possible source of donor human eyes. Maybe if each prisoner donates one eye to the effort, they could get special privaledges.<P>   He was dead serious!||||reg||1
